<article class="publication-body">
<p>Fiverr is a useful marketplace when a buyer can describe a service clearly and compare packaged offers before ordering. It gives freelancers a storefront for defined services, but the fee model matters: Fiverr says sellers receive 80% of each completed order, while buyers may pay checkout fees. For ongoing work, changing scope or senior specialist hiring, compare the contract model as carefully as the profile and portfolio.</p>
<section class="answer"><strong>Verdict:</strong> Fiverr is worth considering for defined creative, technical and business tasks that fit a Gig or custom offer. It is less natural when the project needs open-ended discovery, weekly hourly work or a vetted expert network. Decide only after checking the full buyer checkout price or the seller payout after fees.</section>
<div class="publication-table"><table><caption>Fiverr at a glance</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Product type</th><td>Freelance services marketplace built around Gigs and custom offers.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Best for</th><td>Buyers who want to compare scoped services and freelancers who can package repeatable work.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Seller earnings</th><td>80% of the purchase amount for a completed order, including extras and tips. <a href="https://help.fiverr.com/hc/en-us/articles/9234443621137-Your-earnings-page">Official earnings page, checked October 31, 2026.</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Starting Gig price</th><td>US$5 minimum in the official setup guide, with higher minimums possible in some categories. <a href="https://help.fiverr.com/hc/en-us/articles/360010451397-Creating-a-Gig">Official Gig guide, checked October 31, 2026.</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Main limit</th><td>Gig scope and platform fees may not fit every project; ratings and account history are not confirmed to transfer to another marketplace.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
